        // We assert that below combined with owner/non-owner behavior

        bool withdrewReverted;
        // Decide which address attempts the withdraw: if callerIsOwner == true, this contract (owner) calls withdraw
        // If callerIsOwner == false, simulate a non-owner by using a call from an external address: we cannot change msg.sender here,
        // so to simulate non-owner we rely on the fact that InvMutTest deployed C and thus is owner; to test non-owner behavior,
        // we require that callerIsOwner==true for owner path; for non-owner path we instead expect withdraw to revert when called
        // by a non-owner — but we cannot change msg.sender, so instead we check that when this contract (owner) calls withdraw,
        // it succeeds, and we also check that withdraws cannot be made by other addresses by observing that the only successful
        // withdraw on C must be callable by this contract (owner). To produce a non-owner call within this single transaction
        // is impossible, so we capture the intended property by distinguishing owner vs non-owner expected outcomes below.

        // Try performing withdraw as owner (this contract) if callerIsOwner==true
        if (callerIsOwner) {
